// Phase F study harness: the host-only test suite, behind the one entry point.
//
// This is doc 09 work item 5 -- "define how it is installed, discovered and
// invoked from CMake/lit -- a single .lit wrapper so `ninja check-...` remains
// the one entry point". lit discovers *.lit by glob, so this file needs no
// registration; the suite it runs discovers test_*.py by glob for the same
// reason.
//
// No NPU, no XRT, no compilation and no framework. The study tier's schema,
// results I/O, smoke gate, manifest, iron adapter and ladder shape override are
// all pure host logic, so they gate here rather than waiting for a device.
// These modules run as plain scripts -- collectible by pytest unchanged, but
// depending on none of it. Same choice, and the same reasoning, as
// run_block_cache_tests.lit.
//
// `[2026-08-14]` The reason given here used to be "pytest is not installed in
// the sandbox venv", and that is no longer true: queue item 11(b) installed
// matplotlib, pandas, seaborn and pytest in an exclusive window. The CHOICE to
// use no framework is unchanged, and the second half of the original sentence
// is why -- installing into the venv the gates run from is a hazard whenever a
// gate or a measurement is live, so no module here may depend on a TEST
// framework being present.
//
// One dependency IS now real and is called out rather than hidden:
// `test_plots.py` imports `study/plots.py`, which imports matplotlib at module
// level (it must select the Agg backend before pyplot, since gates run with no
// display). `study/requirements.txt` has declared matplotlib for this tier
// since Phase F, so this is a declared dependency finally being installed, not
// a new one. On a host without it the suite does NOT silently shrink: the
// runner reports `FAILURE test_plots.py: import failed: ImportError`, exits
// non-zero, AND the total below stops matching -- measured at 524/525, so both
// signals fire independently. That is the documented behaviour ("a module that
// fails to import is a failure, not a skip"), and it is the right one here.
//
// THE COUNT IS PINNED ON PURPOSE. Discovery alone cannot catch a test that
// stops being *defined*: delete a test function and a globbing runner happily
// reports a smaller suite passing. Convention rule 11 exists because iron's
// pytest.ini collected none of its own study/test_*.py by directory, and that
// hid real bugs. So the module count and the test count are both matched
// exactly, and a suite that quietly shrinks fails here instead of passing.
//
// Update the numbers below when you add tests. That edit is the point, not
// friction to route around with a looser pattern.
//
