// `[2026-08-14]` 539 -> 556 and 24 -> 25 modules, queue item 31: the
// declaration-side cost bridge (study/analytical_cost.py). Doc 53 section 8
// established that row 31's selector cannot be composed from the parts the row
// names -- doc 48's predicate reads a DECLARATION, doc 47's instrument reads a
// ROUTED ARTIFACT, so they sit on opposite sides of the compile -- and left the
// choice of approximation to the operator. The operator chose an analytical
// model, and this is it.
//
// +20 test_analytical_cost, and the first nine are the reason to believe the
// rest. The traffic half is NOT modelled: it is doc 31a's DRAM-crossing lens
// plus doc 53 section 6's band-serial term, and section 6 derived those figures
// BY HAND off measured artifacts (devq 338/340). This module reproduces every
// one of them TO THE BYTE from a formula written off the mechanism instead --
// weights once, weights per band at both lengths, both tail rows, both net
// rows, the 83-row / 1.30-band crossover, and the 1.52x / 1.77x
// tail-versus-packaged-layer ratios. Two independent derivations agreeing to
// the byte is what makes this a bridge rather than a guess, and the
// discrimination control is that the floor and band-serial forms must COINCIDE
// at one band and DIVERGE from the second on -- otherwise a formula wrong
// everywhere else could still match every figure above.
//
// THE PROVENANCE CLAUSE IS THE ONE TO KEEP. Doc 47's ERT is 1,208 measured / 5
// counted / **0 modelled**, and it declined to import iron's cross-toolchain
// bandwidth figure precisely so that ports report `unpriced` rather than
// ranking against a constant wearing a measurement's label. An analytical model
// introduces this project's first modelled numbers, so they live in
// analytical_cost's own table and the test asserts by `ast` that the module
// does not IMPORT balance_ert at all. It reads the imports rather than
// searching the text because the docstring says "nothing in this file writes
// into balance_ert", and a substring scan flags the very sentence documenting
// the property it checks -- which it did, on first writing.
//
// The rate half is where the modelling is, and it is labelled per term: a
// single-port cost is `measured` (doc 47's back-solve, 655,360 B / 122.81 us ->
// 5.336 GB/s, devq 293) and a multi-port one is `modelled`, because it scales
// that figure LINEARLY while doc 33's imported ladder is non-monotonic and
// peaks at 4 shim tiles rather than 8. So the model over-credits wide designs
// and says so in the term's own provenance string.
//
// RUN: python3 %S/study/run_host_tests.py | FileCheck %s
// Three of the twenty are the MEASURED BOUND on what the module may claim, and
// they are the most useful thing in it. Against the standing four-mode
// comparison: a traffic-only time under-predicts measured latency by 9.7x to
// 20.3x, so an absolute `ns` from here is not a latency; and **byte order does
// not predict latency order** -- by bytes at 1024 it is runlist < fused <
// coarse < offload, by latency fused < coarse < runlist < offload, because
// `runlist` moves the FEWEST bytes and pays 24-32 hw_context loads.
// Reconfiguration is the taxonomy's other axis and this module does not model
// it. What licenses the module at all is the third clause: the bytes-to-time
// ratio is stable WITHIN a mode (coarse 1.02, fused 1.10, runlist 1.03) and the
// between-mode spread is larger than any of them, so the licence is exactly
// "same dispatch structure, different mapping". `offload` is the outlier at
// 1.37 and is bounded rather than smoothed in, because it is the measured
// signature of the axis being left out -- its bytes grow 2.25x from 512 to 1024
// while its latency grows 1.64x.
//
